\relax 
\providecommand\zref@newlabel[2]{}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces A diagram showing an idealised planar fault. The hypocentre is the point on the fault where rupture initiates, while the epicentre is simply the projection of this point onto the Earth’s surface. It is the hypocentre location that can be estimated by looking at the arrival times of high-frequency seismic waves recorded on seismometers.\relax }}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:fig1}{{1}{1}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces A vertical component seismogram recorded in Finland following an earthquake in California. The p-wave arrival can be seen very clearly, while later arriving phases are significantly harder to identify.\relax }}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fig2}{{2}{3}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces A diagram showing some of the many possible ray paths within the Earth. Note in particular that the sudden reduction in p-wave velocity when entering the fluid outer core leads to the so-called “p-wave shadow zone”, while no s-waves are able to propagate within this region.\relax }}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fig3}{{3}{5}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Travel time curves determined for a large number of seismic phases within the Earth. The nomenclature for the different phases can be ignored.\relax }}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fig4}{{4}{8}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Variation of p-wave velocity (black curve) and s-wave velocity (red curve) with radius in the model PREM of Dziewonski \& Anderson (1981). Here we can see that both wave speeds generally increase with depth, but this increase is interrupted by a number of sharp velocity discontinuities. Such discontinuities are associated with compositional or phase changes within the Earth. The most notable boundaries within the Earth are between the the rocky mantle and the fluid iron outer core where the s-wave velocity becomes equal to zero, and at the inner core boundary where the iron of the core undergoes a fluid to solid phase transformation.\relax }}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fig5}{{5}{9}}
